{"qid":"Q4712","names":["Duchy of Burgundy"],"title":"Duchy of Burgundy","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Duchy of Burgundy originated as a Carolingian grant in 880 and became a French royal apanage in 1032; it rose to pan-European prominence under the Valois dukes Philip the Bold (r. 1363–1404), John the Fearless, Philip the Good, and Charles the Bold, who assembled a composite territory stretching from Burgundy proper through Flanders, Artois, Holland, and Brabant. Philip the Good made Dijon a center of Northern Renaissance art patronage and in 1430 founded the Order of the Golden Fleece. Charles the Bold's death at the Battle of Nancy (5 January 1477) ended the line; Louis XI of France annexed the duchy itself while the Low Countries passed by marriage to the Habsburgs through Mary of Burgundy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The duchy's core territories are well-documented; the full extent of the Valois Burgundian state varied substantially by decade as acquisitions and inheritances accumulated, making a single boundary map a simplification.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Duchy of Burgundy","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Burgundy-historical-region-France"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Burgundy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Burgundy"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Burgundy","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Richard the Justiciar","nativeName":"Richard le Justicier","roman":"Richard the Justiciar","start":880,"end":921,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Rudolph (Raoul)","nativeName":"Raoul","roman":"Rudo